\description{
Computes the productivity-based selection indices that plant breeders and
stress physiologists have used for decades, from the performance of each
genotype under control (\eqn{Y_p}) and under stress (\eqn{Y_s}) conditions.

These indices answer a different question from \code{\link[=calculate_sri]{calculate_sri()}}. The Stress
Response Index describes the \emph{physiological signature} across a whole trait
panel; the tolerance indices summarize a \emph{single productivity trait} and are
the currency in which selection decisions are usually written. Both views are
offered so that a physiological ranking can be checked against the agronomic
one.
}
\details{
Let \eqn{\bar{Y}_p} and \eqn{\bar{Y}_s} be the trial means across units.
The available indices are:

\describe{
\item{\code{TOL}}{\eqn{Y_p - Y_s}. Yield loss. Lower is better.}
\item{\code{MP}}{\eqn{(Y_p + Y_s)/2}. Mean productivity. Higher is better.}
\item{\code{GMP}}{\eqn{\sqrt{Y_p Y_s}}. Geometric mean productivity, less biased
towards the high-potential end than \code{MP}. Higher is better.}
\item{\code{HM}}{\eqn{2 Y_p Y_s / (Y_p + Y_s)}. Harmonic mean. Higher is better.}
\item{\code{SSI}}{\eqn{(1 - Y_s/Y_p) / (1 - \bar{Y}_s/\bar{Y}_p)}. Stress
susceptibility, scaled by the stress intensity of the trial. Lower is
better.}
\item{\code{STI}}{\eqn{Y_p Y_s / \bar{Y}_p^2}. Identifies units that perform
well in both environments. Higher is better.}
\item{\code{YI}}{\eqn{Y_s / \bar{Y}_s}. Yield index. Higher is better.}
\item{\code{YSI}}{\eqn{Y_s / Y_p}. Yield stability. Higher is better.}
\item{\code{RDI}}{\eqn{(Y_s/Y_p) / (\bar{Y}_s/\bar{Y}_p)}. Relative drought
index. Higher is better.}
\item{\code{SSPI}}{\eqn{100 (Y_p - Y_s) / (2 \bar{Y}_p)}. Yield loss as a
percentage of the trial potential. Lower is better.}
}

No single index is correct on its own: \code{TOL} and \code{SSI} reward stability even
when it comes with low potential, while \code{MP} rewards potential even when the
loss is large. \code{rank_mean}, the average rank across the requested indices
after orienting each one towards tolerance, is therefore reported as well, and
\code{rank_overall} ranks the units on it.

The stress intensity of each stress level, \eqn{1 - \bar{Y}_s/\bar{Y}_p}, is
returned in the \code{stress_intensity} column; indices that divide by it are \code{NA}
when a stress level did not reduce the trial mean at all.
